00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:31.760
I'm home.

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:34.120
You're late!

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:37.250
Where have you been and what have 
you been doing at this hour?

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:39.800
I was with my friends, that's all.

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:41.280
That again?

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:45.250
Honestly. I've even hired a tutor for you.

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:48.020
But you're just as stupid 
as ever, aren't you?

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:49.910
I give up.

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:56.170
Oh, he makes me hyper-angry, 
that crappy old man!

00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:58.400
Treating me like I'm an idiot...!

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:02.500
You being in the idiot category 
is an objective truth!

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:03.840
More importantly,

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:07.170
solve this Monkey-Hunter problem!

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:08.920
Sorries.

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:13.410
Stop it, monkey! Don't shoot, monkey!

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:16.860
"When Sarukichi, up in a tree, 
is shot at with a gun..."

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:19.840
"Sarukichi is startled by the 
sound of the gunshot and falls."

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:22.630
"Will Sarukichi be hit by the bullet?"

00:03:22.630 --> 00:03:25.560
I feel so bad for Sarukichi, I wanna hurl!

00:03:25.970 --> 00:03:27.960
Enough of that. 
Hurry up and solve the problem.

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:29.820
Will do.

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:37.190
So, the distance Sarukichi is 
falling is one half gt squared...

00:03:37.190 --> 00:03:39.810
I suppose that the fact you can 
now quickly translate problems

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:41.810
into equations like that shows your growth.

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:45.450
Right? Haru's growing day by day!

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:48.300
Why aren't you studying, you imbecile?!

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:49.710
But every time my papa shows up, 
he digs in and starts nagging!

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:51.480
And he smells like an old person!

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:54.010
Seriously, buzz off! Hurry up and go bald!

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:56.580
Do not make light of Professor Kagurano!

00:03:57.060 --> 00:03:59.230
The person who made 
revolutionary contributions

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:02.750
in the method of analyzing 
chronological data is your father,

00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:05.020
Professor Kagurano Kaihou!

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:08.430
He filtered missing values,

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:12.650
designed a special state space 
model that separates noise out,

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:16.570
and opened up new horizons 
in the field of mathematics!

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:21.550
When I read his ingenious paper, 
it touched my heart.

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:25.480
Yukipii-sensei, you're on my dad's side, too?

00:04:26.710 --> 00:04:27.990
Did you say something?

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:32.950
I know that. 
I know Papa's an amazing person.

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:37.080
That's why I'm sure what Papa 
says must be correct, right?

00:04:38.590 --> 00:04:42.380
Hey, Yukipii-sensei. 
Do you worry about stuff?

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:45.800
Or, 'cause you're smart, do you 
solve everything in an instant?

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:48.010
Of course not. Are you stupid?

00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:53.730
If you're asking about problems 
which I must consider...

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:54.780
then, yes, I have those.

00:04:56.800 --> 00:04:57.780
Wha...

00:04:58.840 --> 00:04:59.900
What is it?

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:03.840
Hey, could they be, 
by any chance... romance-related?

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:04.910
What?!

00:05:04.910 --> 00:05:07.160
Tell me! I'm super-curious!

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:09.350
No. It's got nothing to do with you!

00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:12.190
Oh, come on! What's it gonna hurt? 
Tell me just a little!

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:14.170
Yukipii!

00:05:16.550 --> 00:05:18.450
Oh, Yukimura-kun.

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:21.290
Are you already done for today?

00:05:21.730 --> 00:05:23.410
Professor Kagurano!

00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:25.500
Yukipii-sensei?

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:28.060
There's a problem I forgot to ask you about.

00:05:28.290 --> 00:05:30.800
<i>Ugh! Papa's here.</i>

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:34.550
I'm sorry to have you tutor for me.

00:05:34.930 --> 00:05:36.080
Not at all.

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:38.940
If I can be of use to you, 
Professor Kagurano, then...

00:05:40.230 --> 00:05:41.680
Thank you. That's a great help.

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:44.450
So, what do you think of my daughter?

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:48.380
Her fundamental academic skills are lacking,

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:50.670
but she's earnest and she 
absorbs things quickly.

00:05:50.670 --> 00:05:52.670
She's such an idiot, 
you're at your wit's end, aren't you?

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:58.120
Her average score on her last 
end-of-term test was 38 points!

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:00.960
All I can think is that she's got the brains 
of an elementary or middle schooler.

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:06.330
I'm amazed she's survived to this point. 
It's impressive, in its own way.

00:06:06.830 --> 00:06:11.490
Honestly, how could someone so 
stupid be born to my family?

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:12.840
It's a mystery.

00:06:13.420 --> 00:06:16.140
She's been worthless like 
that for a long time.

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:19.590
And now even the way she looks reflects 
how poorly her brain functions.

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:22.370
I know it will be a hardship for you,

00:06:22.370 --> 00:06:25.810
but if she can learn even a little from 
seeing a brilliant student like you, then...

00:06:26.710 --> 00:06:28.240
Yukimura-kun?

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:30.250
Professor Kagurano,

00:06:30.790 --> 00:06:33.360
have you heard of the Pygmalion effect?

00:06:35.290 --> 00:06:39.350
It's a study from 1964 conducted 
by Robert Rosenthal and others.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:43.330
Students in an elementary school 
class were selected at random.

00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:46.740
"These students have the ability 
to greatly improve their grades."

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:49.880
That was the false information 
the teachers were given.

00:06:50.230 --> 00:06:53.760
And the selected students' 
grades did improve.

00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:58.030
The expectations the teachers had of the 
students, the ways they interacted,

00:06:58.030 --> 00:06:59.410
changes in the way they taught them...

00:06:59.790 --> 00:07:03.270
It is conceivable that these things had 
an effect on the students' growth.

00:07:04.070 --> 00:07:07.270
Have you heard of Jane Elliott's 
discrimination experiments?

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:11.530
One day, Elliott, a schoolteacher in 
her elementary school classroom...

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:15.030
Brown-eyed children are superior 
to blue-eyed children.

00:07:15.030 --> 00:07:16.530
...is what she told them,

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:19.540
and began giving the brown-eyed 
students preferential treatment.

00:07:19.540 --> 00:07:26.800
Waaahhhh!

00:07:19.540 --> 00:07:26.800
Yay! Yay!

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:23.680
When she did, the brown-eyed students' 
attitudes toward the blue-eyed students...

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:26.550
became ill-tempered and unkind.

00:07:27.030 --> 00:07:30.700
Later, Elliott told them exactly the 
opposite of what she'd said previously.

00:07:31.160 --> 00:07:34.540
Actually, it's the blue-eyed 
children who are superior.

00:07:34.930 --> 00:07:36.540
And this time,

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:41.690
it was the blue-eyed students who began 
persecuting the brown-eyed students.

00:07:42.340 --> 00:07:44.580
What is discrimination?

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:47.890
The experiment was meant to help 
the children comprehend it.

00:07:47.890 --> 00:07:49.570
And, in truth...

00:07:49.780 --> 00:07:51.790
the students who were 
discriminated against...

00:07:51.790 --> 00:07:54.200
for only the duration of time in which 
they were discriminated against...

00:07:54.200 --> 00:07:57.440
had a huge drop in their 
academic achievements.

00:07:58.540 --> 00:07:59.700
Which means that...

00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:02.960
<b>Outstanding</b>

00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:02.960
<b>All-rounder</b>

00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:02.960
<b>Can do anything</b>

00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:02.960
<b>Amazing</b>

00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:02.960
<b>Genius</b>

00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:02.960
<b>Smart</b>

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:02.970
when people are told they are outstanding, 
they become outstanding,

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:07.090
<b>Moron</b>

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:07.090
<b>Incompetent</b>

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:07.090
<b>Idiot</b>

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:07.090
<b>Stupid</b>

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:07.090
<b>Unskilled</b>

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:07.090
<b>Can't do anything</b>

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:07.090
<b>Useless person</b>

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:07.080
and when they're continuously told they're 
idiots, they truly become idiots.

00:08:08.330 --> 00:08:11.340
What are you trying to say, Yukimura-kun?

00:08:11.560 --> 00:08:13.680
It's those words and actions of yours

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:16.610
that have stifled that woman's brilliance! 
That's what I'm saying!

00:08:16.900 --> 00:08:18.590
Be careful of what you say!

00:08:23.930 --> 00:08:27.090
Apologies. I've said too much.

00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:30.480
I will come again next week.

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:34.660
If you intend to dismiss me, 
please contact me prior to that.

00:08:40.150 --> 00:08:41.620
What was that?

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:45.410
I mean, you call me stupid 
all the time, don't you?

